.TH SHSELECT 1 "2026-11-17" "shselect 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shselect \- terminal selector and pager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shselect
[\fB\-bcghmnsv\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR \fIlines\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fItext\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shselect
is a pager and selector. It reads input from a pipe or stdin and
displays the content in an interactive terminal interface. The user
can move through the list, select entries, and return the selected
line(s) or line number(s) to the calling script.
.PP
The tool is intended for use in POSIX shell scripts where an
interactive selector is useful. Depending on the options,
.B shselect
can return selected lines, their line numbers, or both. When
multiselect mode is enabled, multiple entries may be marked and
returned.
.PP
.B shselect
is part of the shui-tools collection.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Display the bottom bar in reverse video.
.TP
.B \-c
Show the cursor in the first column instead of a reverse-video line.
.TP
.B \-g
Disable the bottom bar.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage information.
.TP
.B \-m
Enable multiselect mode. When enabled, entries may be marked or
unmarked.
.TP
.B \-n
Return only the line number of the selected entry.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fItext\fR
Set the bottom bar text.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR \fIlines\fR
Set the height of the selection area in lines.
A value of 0 uses the entire terminal height. The default is 4.
.TP
.B \-s
Return only the selected line itself.
By default (without \fB\-n\fR or \fB\-s\fR), both the line number
and the line content are returned.
.TP
.B \-v
When exiting with \fBx\fR or \fBq\fR, also return the line the cursor
was on.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While viewing a list, the following keys are available:
.RS 4
.TP 19
.B Right, PageDown
Move forward one window.
.TP
.B Left, PageUp
Move backward one window.
.TP
.B Down
Move down one line.
.TP
.B Up
Move up one line.
.TP
.B Home
Move to the first line.
.TP
.B End
Move to the last line.
.TP
.B Enter
Select and exit.
.TP
.B q
Exit without selection (return 0).
.TP
.B x
Exit without selection (return 10).
.TP
.B m, s, +
Toggle mark (multiselect mode only).
.RE
.PP
Exiting with \fBx\fR returns 10 and can be used as a \(lqgo back\(rq
signal in scripts.
.SH EXAMPLES
Select a file from a directory listing:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ls \-a . | shselect \-s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select from a list of strings:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
printf "%s\en%s\en%s\en" "One" "Two" "Three" | shselect \-sb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Possible usage in a shell script:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
key=$(printf "${radiostations}" | shselect \-bs)
.fi
.RE
